@kind(writ)

=[ Writ: workspace root ]

Rules the whole repository obeys.

Scope is this directory and everything under it. A narrower rule lives in a
`.writ.tmt` closer to what it governs; read every `.writ.tmt` from a file up
to here, the way `.gitignore` is read.

Only the author edits this file. An agent that finds a rule in its way
proposes an edit here and stops. It does not work around the rule, and it
does not relax the rule to fit the change it wanted to make.

==[ crate-layering ]

@rule(crate-layering){
  guard: { twrit: layers }

  layers: {
    6: list( "apps/*", "bindings/*", "editors/*", "tests", "crates/tomet" )
    5: list( "crates/tomet-workspace*", "crates/tomet-load" )
    4: list( "crates/tomet-format*", "crates/tomet-convert-*", "crates/tomet-transform", "crates/serde_tomet", "crates/tomet-extract", "crates/tomet-search", "crates/tomet-vault" )
    3: list( "crates/tomet-config" )
    2: list( "crates/tomet-semantics*" )
    1: list( "crates/tomet-syntax-*" )
    0: list( "crates/tree-sitter-tomet" )
  }
}

Every workspace member belongs to exactly one layer, and depends only on
layers strictly below its own. Same-layer dependencies are allowed;
upward ones are not.

Layer 0 depends on no workspace member at all. Things may depend on it --
`tests` and @dir(apps/tui) both do -- but it may depend on nothing, because it
is the hand-maintained grammar approximation and is only worth having while
it stays independent of the parser.

@dir(crates/serde_tomet), @dir(crates/tomet-extract) and @dir(crates/tomet-search) are listed by name in layer 4. They are ecosystem
adapters and pure in-memory query engines rather than internal syntax or semantics producers, and their names carry no prefix shared
with the producer groups.

@dir(crates/tomet-vault) is likewise listed by name: it is the disk-free half of what `tomet-load` assembles (a vault's config and vocabularies, parse, bind, prepare), so that a caller with no filesystem can use it. `tomet-load` sits above it and adds the reading.

@dir(crates/tomet) is the public facade over every layer below it. It sits in layer 6 with the applications, is listed by name because its name shares no prefix with the crates it re-exports, and holds no logic of its own.

A pattern ending in `*` matches by prefix; every other pattern is exact.
Patterns are directory paths relative to the workspace root, because the
directory is the half of a crate's identity that is arranged by layer --
@dir(crates/tomet-syntax-parser) publishes `tomet-parser`.

Dev-dependencies are exempt. A crate may reach upward in `[dev-dependencies]`
to get a parser for its own tests -- `tomet-syntax-tree` and
`tomet-semantics` both do, and neither is a layering violation.

---[ Why ]---

The order is what keeps a change local. Touching the parser today means
reading `crates/tomet-syntax-*` and nothing else; the moment an edge runs
upward, it means reading the applications too.

The layer is derived from the directory name, not the package name. The two
disagree -- @dir(crates/tomet-syntax-parser) publishes `tomet-parser` -- and the
directory is the half that is arranged by layer, which is why the workspace
member list in `Cargo.toml` is already grouped this way in comments.

Every member's directory now agrees with its layer. It did not always:
@dir(crates/tomet-config) was `crates/tomet-workspace-config`, which read as
part of the workspace layer while the format and convert crates were
sitting on top of it. The rule carried it as a named exception until the
rename, rather than quietly filing it under the wrong prefix -- an
exception a reader can see is worth more than a tidy table that lies.

The rejected alternative is a rule per crate. It states the same thing and
cannot answer the question that matters -- whether some crate has no rule at
all. Deriving the layer from the directory prefix means a new member that
matches no prefix and no exception line above is unclassified, and an
unclassified member is a failure rather than a silence.

---[ Guard ]---

`twrit check`. It reads `@layers` above, reads every workspace member's
`[dependencies]` through `cargo_metadata`, and fails on a member matching
no layer, a member matching two, an upward edge, or anything `crates/
tree-sitter-tomet` comes to depend on.

==[ tmtroot-mirrors-the-root ]

@rule(tmtroot-mirrors-the-root){
  guard: { runs: "just docs-check" }
}

@dir(tmtroot/) holds sources whose output lands at a path this repository does
not choose. Its structure mirrors the repository root: `tmtroot/x.tmt`
produces a file at `/`, `tmtroot/.github/x.tmt` one under `/.github/`.

The `/` in that sentence is the project root -- the directory holding
`default.config.tmt` -- because that is what a declared output path is
measured from, wherever the export is run from.

Nothing else goes there. A document whose output lands beside it -- the
ordinary case -- stays where it belongs by what it is, and @dir(docs/)'s own
writ decides that.

---[ Why ]---

`README.ja.md` has to be at the repository root because that is where a
forge looks for it. `AGENTS.md` likewise, because that is where agent
tooling looks. The source cannot sit beside its output without putting two
files with the same content side by side, so it has to live somewhere else
-- and then something has to say where the output goes.

Mirroring answers that without a rule anyone has to read. The path is the
answer. Every project that has needed this has arrived at the same shape:
GNU Stow maps `dotfiles/vim/.vimrc` to `~/.vimrc`, chezmoi mirrors `$HOME`,
Hugo copies `static/robots.txt` to `/robots.txt`, `public/favicon.ico`
becomes `/favicon.ico` in most web toolchains, and a Debian package builds
`debian/<pkg>/usr/bin/x` for `/usr/bin/x`.

The name has precedent too. `sysroot`, `buildroot` and `chroot` all name a
directory standing in for `/`, and `tmtroot` is the same compound. Plain
`root/` was rejected for sitting inside the actual root and reading as a
claim to be it; `exports/` for describing every export rather than these,
since a document under `spec/` may export perfectly well and stay put.

One place it is weaker than the prior art: those copy verbatim, so the
output name is visible in the source name. Here `@config(export: path)`
renames, and @file(tmtroot/readme.tmt) becoming `/README.ja.md` cannot be read
off the path. Deriving the path from the position when it is not declared
would close that, and is not decided.

What a declared path is measured from was decided, and it was not a
design question in the end. `export` handed the string to `fs::write`
unchanged, so it resolved against the process's working directory:
@file(tmtroot/readme.tmt) landed at the repository root or under @dir(docs/)
depending on where you stood, and this rule held only because everyone
happened to run from the root. The answer already existed -- a path
written inside a document is absolute, or `./`/`../` from the document's
own directory, or measured from the project root -- and links had been
following it all along. Export was the one place that had not. The rule
now lives in `tomet-indexer::resolve_document_relative` so there is one
copy of it, and `a_bare_export_path_is_measured_from_the_project_root`
pins it.

---[ Guard ]---

`tomet export --check .` covers whether the outputs are current, and is
green -- it is measured from the project root now, so it means the same
thing from any directory.

The other half -- that every `.tmt` under @dir(tmtroot/) declares an export at
all, since a file there that produces nothing is a file in the wrong
directory:

```bash
find tmtroot -name '*.tmt' -exec grep -L 'export:' {} +
```

Empty while every file declares one. Both halves run in `just docs-check`.

It waited for a green day rather than shipping red, which is what a guard
nobody can satisfy teaches people to ignore. `agents.tmt` was empty and
`cluade.tmt` was empty and misspelled; the first now exports `AGENTS.md`
and the second is deleted, because `CLAUDE.md` is eleven bytes of tooling
glue whose whole content is `@AGENTS.md` -- a line Tomet reads as an
element in namespace `AGENTS` and rejects.

A grep and not a parse. It can be fooled by prose containing `export:`,
which fails open, and the honest reason it is a grep is that no tool
reports "this document declares no output": `tomet export` prints such a
file to stdout and `--check` only compares outputs that were declared.
Tighten it when one does.

==[ tmtroot-output-not-edited ]

@rule(tmtroot-output-not-edited){
  guard: { runs: "just docs-check" }
}

A file exported from @dir(tmtroot/) is generated. Do not hand-edit it. Edit
the source and re-export.

Each source says so in its own output, from the macro rather than by
hand:

```tmt
$macro.generated_by(self.path)
```

`.gitattributes` marks `/AGENTS.md` and `/README.ja.md`
`linguist-generated`. `README.md` is not marked, and is not generated: it
is hand-written English, and only its Japanese sibling comes from
@dir(tmtroot/).

---[ Why ]---

`docs/.writ.tmt` has the same rule for @dir(docs/), and a writ is scoped to
its own directory, so that one cannot reach a file at the repository root.
The rule has to exist here or `AGENTS.md` is governed by nothing.

`AGENTS.md` is the case that made it urgent. It is what every agent
working in this repository reads, so it is the file most likely to be
edited in place by something that never looked at @dir(tmtroot/) -- and the
edit would be lost at the next export, silently.

The banner is written `${macro.generated_by(self.path)}` and not typed
out because typing it puts the source's own path inside the source, where
it goes stale the moment the file moves. The wording lives once, in
`default.config.tmt`.

---[ Guard ]---

`tomet export --check .`, the same one as above: a hand-edit makes the
output disagree with its source, which is exactly what that reports.

==[ dot-tomet-is-authored ]

@rule(dot-tomet-is-authored){
  guard: { runs: "just writ-check" }
}

@dir(.tomet/) is the directory Tomet defines for itself inside a vault, and
only what a person writes goes in it -- configuration, blueprints,
anything else this format asks an author for. It is committed. Nothing a
tool generates goes there, or anywhere else in the tree: derived state
belongs in the user's cache directory, `~/.cache/tomet/` on Unix.

---[ Why ]---

A leading dot says the directory was defined by an application rather than
by whoever made the vault. It says nothing about who writes what is inside
it. `.cargo/`, `.vscode/` and `.zed/` are hand-written settings;
`.direnv/` is a built environment. So @dir(.tomet/) being hidden was never an
argument for putting machine state in it -- and the two were mixed here
until this entry: `find_template` looked up `.tomet/templates/` while
`.gitignore` swallowed @dir(.tomet/) whole, which made it a directory you
could be told to write to and could never commit.

The in-tree directories that do hold generated state are not there because
the state is generated. `target/`, `result`, `node_modules/` and `.venv/`
are there because something resolves those exact paths -- a linker, a
runtime's module lookup, an interpreter. Position is the reason, not
derivation. pnpm makes the distinction visible: it moved `node_modules/`'s
contents into a global store and left behind only the symlinks whose
position means something.

A link cache has no such requirement, and a vault gives it a reason to be
elsewhere. A vault is synced -- Dropbox, iCloud, git -- and a SQLite file
inside a synced directory is a binary conflict waiting to happen. That is
what separates a vault from a project: `.mypy_cache/` is never synced, a
vault usually is. Obsidian's `.obsidian/` is where this goes wrong in
practice.

Rejected, in the order they were argued:

- Ignoring `.tomet/cache/` and committing the rest. It spells the ignore
  more precisely and changes nothing else: the mixing stays, and a
  database still sits inside a tree that gets synced.
- Keeping the cache vault-local because it costs one `.gitignore` line,
  the same as `.git/`, `target/` and `node_modules/`. That was the
  reasoning in `cache_path.rs`, and it does not survive being checked --
  those three are entirely machine-owned, which is exactly the property
  @dir(.tomet/) did not have.
- Putting the writ blueprint at the repository root, on the principle
  that configuration and schema sit at the root of what they govern. The
  root is a table of contents, not a classification. What earns a place
  there is the index itself (`README.md`), what an outside tool positions
  (`Cargo.toml`, `flake.nix`, `justfile`, `AGENTS.md`), and what this
  repository's own conventions position (`.writ.tmt`, one per directory).
  A blueprint is none of the three.

`default.config.tmt` stays at the root, and not by the principle just
rejected. `find_config_file` returns the directory it was found in as
`config_root`, so its position is what declares where a vault begins: it
is a boundary marker, like `Cargo.toml` or `package.json`. Making the
directory the marker instead -- `.tomet/config.tmt`, the way `.git/`
works -- is coherent and undecided, and is held up by the four spellings
the search already accepts.

---[ Guard ]---

```bash
git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard .tomet
```

Empty while nothing under @dir(.tomet/) is ignored. It catches the failure
that actually happened -- a rule telling authors to write somewhere git
was told to discard -- rather than trying to prove no tool ever writes
into the tree.

==[ blueprint-shape ]

@rule(blueprint-shape){
  guard: { none: "checkable in two halves, neither written yet" }
}

A blueprint declares what it is, what it targets, and what it says about
itself -- in that order, in the three slots every element has.

```tmt
@kind(blueprint)
@blueprint(writ){
  version: "1.0.0"
  description: "twrit"
}
```

`@kind(blueprint)` is written, never inferred from `@blueprint` being
present. The target is the positional argument, normalized to `target`.
`@blueprint`'s `{...}` holds the blueprint's own metadata and never the
target. Instantiating promotes `@blueprint(X)` to `@kind(X)`.

---[ Why ]---

`why/root.tmt` puts it plainly: identifier, input, data, and a setting that
says where each one goes. A blueprint has all three and nothing else --
what it is, what it is parameterised by, what it records about itself. The
shape is not invented for blueprints; it is the shape, applied.

`@kind` is the one answer to "what is this document". Inferring it from an
element's presence makes a second path to the same question, and a second
path is what has gone wrong every time: `@meta{type:}` beside `@kind`, the
identifier rules differing between element arguments and interpolation, `-`
and `#` taking different groups from `@name`.

Rejected, and worth naming because the code accepted all of them at once:

- `@blueprint(kind: writ)`. Both spellings behave identically:
  `document_kind` narrows to `@kind` elements with `classify_lenient`
  before it reads any argument, so a blueprint's key is never mistaken for
  the document's own kind whatever it is called. This is a choice for the
  reader, not a collision being avoided -- and worth saying so, because a
  rule that looks like it has a mechanical reason sends the next person
  hunting for one. What it costs is that `kind` would appear twice in
  three lines meaning two different things: this file's kind, and the
  output's.
- `@blueprint(target: writ)`. Spelling the key is not wrong, only
  redundant: `embed` and `link` already normalize a positional to `target`,
  and `@kind(writ)` is positional too, so promotion becomes the same value
  moving rather than a change of shape.
- `@target(writ)` as a separate element. It makes the promotion easy to
  see, but the target is a fact *about the blueprint* and belongs with its
  other facts. One element fewer.
- A document counting as a blueprint because it has any `@kind` at all.
  That is what `extract_blueprint_schema` did, and it is why nothing could
  tell a blueprint from the documents it describes.

None of this was written down when `@blueprint` was implemented in
`bb38aff`, and it appears nowhere in @dir(docs/spec/). That is the whole reason
this entry exists.

---[ Guard ]---

None yet. Two halves are checkable: no document carries `@blueprint`
without `@kind(blueprint)`, and `@blueprint`'s args are a bare positional
rather than a `target:`/`kind:` map.

==[ explicit-form-first ]

@rule(explicit-form-first){
  guard: { none: "no mechanical check for the general rule" }
}

Build the explicit form first. A shorthand comes afterward, as an
alternative that expands to it, and nothing is ever written twice.

The test that separates the two:

- A **shorthand** expands to the explicit form. `tomet fmt` can do it
  mechanically, because the explicit form holds everything the shorthand
  meant.
- A **duplicate** restates the explicit form. Nothing can repair it,
  because when the two disagree there is no ground for saying which one
  is right.

If a second spelling cannot be expanded into the first, it is not a
shorthand, whatever it is called.

@file(docs/why/dev.tmt) states the same thing as a commitment about the
language, for the people using it. This entry is the working rule: the
test above, and what it has already caught here. Neither restates the
other -- if this entry ever starts explaining *why the language is like
that*, the explanation belongs there instead.

---[ Why ]---

Every asymmetry this repository has had to remove was a duplicate that
had been mistaken for a convenience.

- `@meta{ type: }` beside `@kind(...)`. The same fact in two places; by
  the time anyone counted, eleven files under @dir(docs/) had drifted back to
  the old spelling.
- `@blueprint`'s three argument forms -- positional, `target:`, `kind:`.
  Three ways to say one thing, none of them chosen.
- Three template lookup directories times three filename spellings, which
  is how `docs/docs.settings.tmt` pointed at a file that does not exist
  and still resolved: the misses were invisible and one hit.
- `required: [ name ]` and `positional: [ name ]` hoisted out of an
  `args` map. The intent was good -- a reader wants those two answers
  without scanning every argument -- but a hand-written summary of
  derivable facts is a duplicate, and `positional` went further by
  stating an order the argument list already had. What a reader wants is
  a *view*, and a view is generated, not stored.

Shorthands, by contrast, have cost nothing:

- `@link` for `@std.link`, and a kind's own names inside a document that
  declares that kind. Both expand, and both resolve without leaving the
  file.
- An argument's position in a declaration, rather than a separate list
  naming the order.

The rule also says which half to build when only one can be afforded: the
explicit one. A shorthand with no explicit form behind it has nowhere to
expand to, and becomes the only spelling by accident rather than by
choice.

---[ Guard ]---

None mechanical for the general rule. The checkable half is per feature:
a shorthand must have a formatter expansion, and a test that the
expansion round-trips. A shorthand `tomet fmt` cannot expand is a second
spelling wearing the word.

==[ kind-not-meta-type ]

@rule(kind-not-meta-type){
  guard: { runs: "tomet refactor --check --meta-kind" }
}

A document declares its kind with a top-level `@kind(...)`. The `type:`
field inside `@meta` is the superseded spelling and does not appear in this
repository -- not in documentation, not in examples, not in fixtures.

---[ Why ]---

A kind selects the schema that validates the document, so it is a directive
about the document, not a field of its metadata. Buried inside `@meta` it
was reachable only through a nested lookup, and a reader had to open a map
to find out what they were holding.

This rule exists because the decision had already been made and tooled --
`promote_meta_type_to_kind`, and a `refactor` subcommand to run it -- and
still lost ground. With no rule naming it, @dir(docs/guide/) went on teaching
the old spelling, @file(docs/spec/builtin-settings.tmt) was itself written in it,
and eleven files under @dir(docs/) had drifted back by the time anyone counted.
The migration existed; the rule did not.

---[ Guard ]---

`tomet refactor --check --meta-kind` -- non-zero while any file still
carries the old spelling.

Known gap: files that fail to parse are skipped with a warning rather than
failing the run, so a broken file is also an unguarded one.

==[ file-io-doors ]

@rule(file-io-doors){
  guard: { twrit: door }

  door: {
    names: list( "std::fs" )
    within: list( "crates/*" )
    allowed: list(
      "crates/tomet-semantics-resolver",
      "crates/tomet-config",
      "crates/tomet-load",
      "crates/tomet-workspace",
      "crates/tomet-workspace-indexer",
      "crates/tomet-workspace-links"
    )
  }
}

A library crate reads a file only if it is named above. Programs are out
of scope: a CLI reads files because that is what a CLI is.
